# Video A, timed against the 154.84s take recorded 2026-08-22.
# Re-time after ANY change to a.tape. Find the spans with
#   ffmpeg -i a.webm -vf freezedetect=n=-60dB:d=3 -map 0:v -f null -
# and check the frames render.sh drops in workflows/a.boundaries/ — and use
# accurate seeking (`-i file -ss t`, in that order) when sampling frames by
# hand: this webm's keyframes are sparse across the static stretches, so
# `-ss` before `-i` silently returns a frame from many seconds earlier.

# `jb new` provisions in silence: freezedetect finds no change at all between
# 6.36s and 21.28s. Starts 0.6s late so the command and its first lines read at
# normal speed, ends 0.28s early so the output resuming does too.
speed 7.0 21.0 6

# The agent working. Verified by accurate frame sampling: its TUI is on screen
# from before 80s until the detach just after 126.2s, it finishes at ~117.8s
# ("Cogitated for 1m 16s") and the last 8.4s are a completely static frame.
# Speeding the whole visit rather than only that idle tail is deliberate — the
# agent's output is a wall of streaming text that no viewer reads at 1× either,
# and 44s of it is the single slowest thing in the video.
speed 81.0 125.0 6

# Act 2's `jb ls`, with the new container in the table under the output that
# created it — the frame that says what the tool is without needing play.
poster 36.0

# A looping excerpt for README.md and the PyPI page, which have no video
# player. Times are in the CUT clip's timeline, not the raw render's: 5.5s is
# where `jb new` is typed and 26s is just after the second `jb ls` shows the
# new container in the table. That window is the whole pitch on its own — one
# container before, one command, one container after — and it carries the ×6
# badge through the provisioning it speeds up.
teaser 5.5 20.5
